<blockquote>
<p>If there's a building that no one can reach, fuck it, now it's a storage site</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Pretty much.</p>
<p>First of all, the approval was tentative/provisional, so they're ready to immediately pull records out of there if it proves to be a bad idea (and they might be doing that now that it's upgraded to Keter).</p>
<p>Second of all, all the data is extremely non-critical: they're <em>old</em> records and none deal with actual SCP data or documentation. It's like how, before digital records, hospitals would have warehouses full of paper files on patients that were seen once a decade ago and never since. The chances of anyone needing this data <strong>RIGHT NOW!!!</strong> is extremely slim, and while it would be annoying if it became totally inaccessible, it wouldn't be a major blow to the Foundation's infrastructure. More akin to losing your car insurance card and having to order a new one.</p>
<p>Third, these are just paper files. If/as they're digitized, they're removed from the buildings, and there are no data servers on-site. Even experimental data about SCP-1698 itself is uploaded off-site by wi-fi or satellite.</p>

